#use(std/code)
#use(std/html)

#section{Backends}::

Functions emit an element IR, not HTML strings; a pluggable backend
serializes it. #code{--backend} selects #code{html} (default, the reference
serialization), #code{markdown} (CommonMark/GFM; constructs with a Markdown
form render natively, the rest falls back to embedded HTML), or #code{typst}
(structural mapping; AsciiMath becomes Typst math)

Raw escape hatches are per-backend functions, clearly non-portable.
For HTML, #code{#use(std/html)} gives inline #code{#html} and block-level
#code{#html-block}:

#code(lang: "plain")
	Prose with inline #html{<strong>raw</strong>} that stays in the paragraph.

	#html-block{<div class="note">a block-level div</div>}

Live:

Prose with inline #html{<strong>raw</strong>} that stays in the paragraph

#html-block{<div class="note">a block-level div</div>}

The difference: inline raw joins the surrounding paragraph flow;
#code{#html-block} breaks the paragraph and stands alone (a #code{<div>}
inside a #code{<p>} would be invalid HTML)

Corner cases:

#list
	#item
		A backend #strong{errors} on raw nodes addressed to a different backend, never skips them silently — this page
		renders only with the HTML backend by design. Portable documents avoid raw escapes entirely
	#item
		The typst raw form is host-level: #code|={#(raw 'typst "#pagebreak()" #t)}=| (the trailing #code{#t} marks it
		block-level)
	#item
		The markdown backend drops presentation-only attributes (#code{id}, #code{class}); the typst backend drops
		#code{id}s and renders fragment links as plain URL links — cross-page anchor targets in those backends are a
		documented limitation
	#item: Unresolved delayed nodes are also a backend error; backends only ever see resolved IR
